answer:
Cancel before multiplying: \begin{align*}
\frac{14!}{5!9!}&=\frac{14\cdot13\cdot12\cdot11\cdot10\cdot9!}{5\cdot4\cdot3\cdot2\cdot9!} \\
&= \frac{14 \cdot 13 \cdot 12 \cdot 11 \cdot 10}{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2} \\
&= \frac{14 \cdot 13 \cdot 12 \cdot 11}{4 \cdot 3} \\
&= 14 \cdot 13 \cdot 11 \\
&= \boxed{2002}
\end{align*}

If $r$ is the radius of the circle, we know that $x = \pi r^2$ and $y = 2\pi r$. Thus, we have \begin{align*}
x + y &= 80\pi\\
\pi r^2 + 2\pi r &= 80\pi\\
r(r + 2) &= 80.
\end{align*}

We want to find two integers whose product is 80, such that one integer is two more than the other. We note that 80 can be factored as $8\cdot 10$. Therefore, $r = \boxed{8}$.

