answer:
Let $O$ be the origin, and let $P,$ $Q,$ $V$ be the points corresponding to vectors $\mathbf{p},$ $\mathbf{q},$ and $\mathbf{v},$ respectively.  Then $\frac{OP}{OV} = \frac{5}{7}.$

[asy]
import olympiad;
unitsize (0.5 cm);

pair O, P, Q, V;

O = (0,0);
P = (5,0);
V = (5,8);
Q = (P + reflect(O,V)*(P))/2;

draw(O--P--V--cycle);
draw(P--Q);
draw(rightanglemark(O,P,V,14));
draw(rightanglemark(P,Q,O,14));

label("$O$", O, SW);
label("$P$", P, SE);
label("$Q$", Q, NW);
label("$V$", V, NE);
[/asy]

Note that right triangles $OQP$ and $OPV$ are similar, so
\[\frac{OQ}{OP} = \frac{OP}{OV} = \frac{5}{7}.\]Then
\[\frac{\|\mathbf{q}\|}{\|\mathbf{v}\|} = \frac{OQ}{OV} = \frac{OQ}{OP} \cdot \frac{OP}{OV} = \boxed{\frac{25}{49}}.\]

Note that $AD = 6,$ $BD = 4,$ and $CD = 2.$  Then by Pythagoras, $AB = 2 \sqrt{13},$ $AC = 2 \sqrt{10},$ and $BC = 2 \sqrt{5}.$  By Heron's Theorem,
\begin{align*}
[ABC]^2 &= (\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(-\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(\sqrt{5} - \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} - \sqrt{13}) \\
&= ((\sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})^2 - (\sqrt{5})^2)((\sqrt{5})^2 - (\sqrt{10} - \sqrt{13})^2) \\
&= (18 + 2 \sqrt{130})(2 \sqrt{130} - 18) \\
&= 196,
\end{align*}so $[ABC] = 14.$

